1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,829
How Precept Ledger works.

2
00:00:02,049 --> 00:00:06,650
A tour of the services, the records they write, and the checks that make each one trustworthy.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,907
Precept Ledger is a few small services around one ledger.

4
00:00:11,127 --> 00:00:14,391
Gate stands in front of every tool call the payments agent makes.

5
00:00:14,611 --> 00:00:21,416
The ledger keeps every decision. Three independent witnesses cosign its checkpoints, and outside time anchors seal them.

6
00:00:21,636 --> 00:00:27,247
People sign in to two web apps: the Console to operate and prove, and the Approvals app to sign.

7
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:32,693
Every call to Muse Spark or Muse Wonder goes through one module, and code checks what comes back.

8
00:00:32,913 --> 00:00:36,883
And the offline verifier checks an export, with nothing but public keys.

9
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:39,156
Follow one tool call.

10
00:00:39,376 --> 00:00:44,731
Gate maps it to an action and typed facts: the amount, whether the payee is new, the local hour.

11
00:00:44,951 --> 00:00:50,071
When delegation is on, it checks a credential signed by the business owner the agent works for.

12
00:00:50,291 --> 00:00:53,981
Then Cedar rules decide: allow, deny, or hold for a person.

13
00:00:54,201 --> 00:00:58,468
Gate signs the decision and appends it. The ledger answers with a signed receipt.

14
00:00:58,688 --> 00:01:05,683
The agent's wrapper checks Gate's signature and the receipt itself, and only then does the tool run. No receipt means deny.

15
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:08,852
A held call opens an approval request.

16
00:01:09,072 --> 00:01:13,829
Muse Spark writes a brief in the background, and every fact in it must cite a ledger record.

17
00:01:14,049 --> 00:01:23,159
A supervisor signs in to the Approvals app and decides. The signed verdict is recorded first. On approval the tool runs, and its result is recorded too.

18
00:01:23,379 --> 00:01:28,483
If nobody decides before the deadline, the request expires, and that counts as a denial.

19
00:01:29,433 --> 00:01:31,417
Every record follows one recipe.

20
00:01:31,637 --> 00:01:36,629
It is serialized as canonical JSON, so the same record always gives the same bytes.

21
00:01:36,849 --> 00:01:42,908
Its producer signs those bytes with Ed25519, before the ledger ever sees them.

22
00:01:43,128 --> 00:01:49,336
The ledger chains it. Each hash covers the previous hash and the record, so any edit breaks at its sequence number.

23
00:01:49,556 --> 00:01:55,283
And it becomes a leaf of a Merkle tree, with its own prefix, so one kind of hash can never pass for another.

24
00:01:56,233 --> 00:02:00,393
Every ten seconds, the ledger signs a checkpoint: the tree size and its root.

25
00:02:00,613 --> 00:02:06,907
Each witness asks for a consistency proof from the last size it signed, checks that history only grew, and cosigns.

26
00:02:07,127 --> 00:02:09,836
Two of three cosignatures make a checkpoint final.

27
00:02:10,056 --> 00:02:16,093
Final checkpoints get an R F C thirty-one sixty-one timestamp, and every hour, an anchor in Bitcoin.

28
00:02:16,313 --> 00:02:22,917
If the operator rewrites history, even with the log's own key, there is no consistency proof, and the witnesses refuse.

29
00:02:23,867 --> 00:02:26,277
Rules begin as the bank's written policy.

30
00:02:26,497 --> 00:02:29,889
Muse Wonder drafts each rule, with the exact quote it came from.

31
00:02:30,109 --> 00:02:40,243
Code compiles the rules to Cedar, runs every fixture, and checks each number against its clause. A fifty thousand dollar limit under a five thousand dollar clause is refused.

32
00:02:40,463 --> 00:02:45,183
A compliance officer approves, and Gate switches to the new rule set, recording its hash.

33
00:02:46,133 --> 00:02:51,851
When an examiner asks a question, the export is verified first, up to the latest witnessed checkpoint.

34
00:02:52,071 --> 00:02:56,231
The model picks the filters, and code runs the query over that proven history.

35
00:02:56,451 --> 00:03:00,525
Muse Spark answers in claims, each citing records and asserting their fields.

36
00:03:00,745 --> 00:03:06,917
The claim checker proves each claim, or rejects it with the exact reason. And the audit itself is recorded.

37
00:03:07,867 --> 00:03:12,645
The Policy Learner counts patterns with SQL, and drops approvals that look rubber-stamped.

38
00:03:12,865 --> 00:03:18,476
Muse Spark drafts a rule change, and must restate every count, which code checks against SQL.

39
00:03:18,696 --> 00:03:24,008
A replay blocks any loosening that would have allowed a past rejection, and loosening needs two approvers.

40
00:03:24,228 --> 00:03:32,150
Muse Wonder can even revise the learner's prompt. Code plants four faults in each draft, every one must still be caught, and two officers approve.

41
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:36,193
People sign in, and each role sees only its own screens.

42
00:03:36,413 --> 00:03:44,243
Supervisors run the agent and approve payments. Compliance officers own the rules. An examiner can read and ask, but never act.

43
00:03:44,463 --> 00:03:49,683
Other roles' pages are refused, and Gate still checks every action with the person's own token.

44
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:52,063
Anyone can check the result.

45
00:03:52,283 --> 00:03:59,749
Export the stream, and the offline verifier checks every signature, the chain, the Merkle root, the witness quorum and the timestamps.

46
00:03:59,969 --> 00:04:05,250
It opens no network connection, and if anything was changed, it names the first broken record.

47
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:07,352
Models extract.

48
00:04:07,572 --> 00:04:08,511
Code decides.

49
00:04:08,731 --> 00:04:09,968
Anyone can verify.

50
00:04:10,188 --> 00:04:11,783
That's how Precept Ledger works.
